a. If the PowerUp has the e:eaponType shield, it increases the shield level by 1.
b. Any other PowerUp e:eaponType will be a weapon, so that is the default case.
c. If the PowerUp is the same e:eaponType as the existing weapons, a search
occurs for an unused weapon slot and an attempt is made to set that empty slot
to the same weapon type. If all five slots are already in use, nothing happens.
d. If the PowerUp is a different e:eaponType, then all weapon slots are cleared,
and the Weapon in Zeapons>@ is set to the new e:eaponType that was
picked up.
tip
A GAME IS A SERIES OF INTERESTING CHOICES In Chapter 1, "Think-
ing Like a Designer," this was listed as Sid Meier's definition of a game. The
else clause at line // d of Code Listing 32.27 resets the number of weap-
ons to 1 any time you pick up a different weapon, which causes the player
to make an interesting decision. Because we will make the Blaster PowerUp
much more common than the Spread PowerUp, it's likely that the player will
have a few Blasters attached to their ship before the first Spread PowerUp
appears, and then they'll have the interesting decision to make of whether
to stick with the power of the multiple Blasters they already have or to
drop down to a single Spread gun, which will be less powerful but start
them down the path to eventually getting five Spread Weapons (which is
extremely powerful). This is only a small bit of code, but its implications on
the game design are huge!
